FoodExpress – Online Food Delivery Order Management Using Queues

Project Overview:
FoodExpress is a Java-based desktop application that simulates an online food delivery ordering system. It provides a customer interface for placing food orders and a restaurant dashboard for managing orders using the Queue data structure.
The project demonstrates the FIFO (First In, First Out) principle, where the first order placed is the first order processed.

Objectives

To implement the Queue data structure in a real-world application.

To demonstrate enqueue and dequeue operations.

To develop a user-friendly graphical interface using Java Swing.

To manage customer orders and track pending and delivered orders.

Features

1. Customer Interface

Displays available food items with prices.

Allows customers to select food items.

Accepts customer names and order quantities.

Generates unique order IDs starting from 101.

Confirms successful order placement.

2. Restaurant Dashboard

Displays the number of pending and delivered orders.

Visualizes the order queue from FRONT to REAR.

Shows order details, including order ID, customer name, food item, and quantity.

Provides an option to process the next order.

Allows users to refresh the queue display.

Supports navigation between the customer view and restaurant dashboard.

3. Queue Management

Enqueue: New orders are added to the rear of the queue.

Dequeue: The next order is removed from the front of the queue.

FIFO: Orders are processed in the same order in which they were placed.

Order Tracking: Processed orders are stored separately in a delivered orders list.

🛠️ Technologies Used

Programming Language: Java

GUI Framework: Java Swing

Layout and Styling: Java AWT

Data Structures: Queue, LinkedList, ArrayList

IDE: Visual Studio Code or any Java-compatible IDE

🧠 Data Structures Used

Queue

A Queue follows the FIFO (First In, First Out) principle. It is used to maintain pending food orders.

The application uses:

Queue<Order> orderQueue = new LinkedList<>();

add() inserts a new order into the queue.

poll() removes and returns the first order.

isEmpty() checks whether the queue contains any orders.

ArrayList

An ArrayList stores orders that have been delivered.

List<Order> deliveredOrders = new ArrayList<>();

Order Class

The Order class stores the details of each order:

Order ID

Customer name

Food item

Quantity

Order status

⚙️ Application Workflow

The customer opens the FoodExpress application.

The customer selects a food item and clicks ORDER NOW.

The customer enters their name and quantity.

The application creates a new order with a unique ID.

The order is added to the rear of the queue.

The restaurant dashboard displays the pending orders.

The restaurant clicks PROCESS NEXT ORDER.

The first order is removed from the queue and marked as delivered.

The dashboard updates the pending and delivered order counts.

